@ Textures \emph{repeat}: the texel to the right of the last column is the first
column. In C++ the remainder of a negative number is negative, hence the double |%|
in |Wrap|.

<<Texel access with wrap-around>>=
static int Wrap(int i, int n) { return ((i % n) + n) % n; }

float MapImage::Texel(int x, int y) const {
    return rgba[(size_t(Wrap(y, height)) * width + Wrap(x, width)) * 4] / 255.0f;
}

@ \emph{Bilinear sampling} must agree with the GPU, so we must know the GPU's
convention: the centre of texel $i$ is at $u=(i+\frac12)/W$. A coordinate $u$ therefore
lies between the texels $x_0=\lfloor uW-\frac12\rfloor$ and $x_0+1$, with weight
$f_x=uW-\frac12-x_0$ for the second one; the same holds vertically. The result
interpolates first along $x$ on two rows and then along $y$.

<<Bilinear sampling on the CPU>>=
XMFLOAT4 MapImage::Sample(float u, float v) const {
    // Same convention as the GPU: texel centers sit at (i + 0.5) / size.
    const float x = u * width - 0.5f, y = v * height - 0.5f;
    const int x0 = int(std::floor(x)), y0 = int(std::floor(y));
    const float fx = x - x0, fy = y - y0;
    float out[4] = {};
    for (int c = 0; c < 4; ++c) {
        auto at = [&](int xi, int yi) {
            return rgba[(size_t(Wrap(yi, height)) * width + Wrap(xi, width)) * 4 + c] / 255.0f;
        };
        const float top = at(x0, y0) * (1 - fx) + at(x0 + 1, y0) * fx;
        const float bot = at(x0, y0 + 1) * (1 - fx) + at(x0 + 1, y0 + 1) * fx;
        out[c] = top * (1 - fy) + bot * fy;
    }
    return {out[0], out[1], out[2], out[3]};
}

